      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A whole house fan and an attic fan are not two versions of the same thing. A whole house fan sits in a ceiling between the living space and the attic, pulls cool evening air in through open windows and pushes the hot house air out through the attic, and it can cool a home for a fraction of what air conditioning costs, in the right climate. An attic fan sits in the roof or a gable and exhausts only the attic. It does nothing directly for your rooms, its energy savings have been small or negative in field testing, and in a leaky house it can pull conditioned air up through the ceiling. For most homes the choice is not close.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. 1. The Short Answer: Different Jobs, and Usually One Clear Winner
          </h2>

          <p>
            The confusion is understandable. Older whole house fans were often called attic fans, both sit in the attic, and both are big fans that move hot air outdoors. But the air they move is different. A whole house fan moves the air in your living space. With windows open, it draws outdoor air through the rooms, up through a ceiling grille and into the attic, where it leaves through the attic vents. A powered attic fan, often called a powered attic ventilator, moves only the air already in the attic, pulling replacement air in through soffit or gable vents and pushing it out through the roof.
          </p>

          <p>
            That difference decides what each one can do for you. A whole house fan can drop the indoor temperature of an entire house on an evening when it is cooler outside than inside, and it flushes the hot attic as a side effect. An attic fan lowers the attic temperature somewhat during the day, which can reduce the heat that conducts down through the ceiling, but it does not exchange a single breath of air in your rooms. If your goal is a cooler house without running the air conditioner, only one of them does that.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Whole house fan</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Attic fan (powered attic ventilator)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">What it moves</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Living-space air, out through the attic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Attic air only</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Where it mounts</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ceiling between house and attic, usually a hallway</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roof deck or gable wall</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">When it runs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Evening and night, when outside is cooler, windows open</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Daytime, on a thermostat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical airflow</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 1,500 to over 6,000 CFM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 600 to 1,600 CFM</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Direct effect on rooms</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Large, when outdoor air is cool</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None; indirect at best</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Main risk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Running it with windows shut</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Depressurising the house and pulling conditioned air</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Our position is straightforward. In a climate with cool, reasonably dry evenings, a whole house fan is one of the most cost-effective cooling upgrades available, and our <a href="/best-whole-house-fan/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best whole house fan</a> rankings cover the models we would buy. An attic fan is rarely worth installing, especially in an air-conditioned house, and the building science research behind that view is laid out in the debate section below. The rest of this guide explains the numbers so you can check the verdict against your own house.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-they-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. 2. How Each Fan Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            A whole house fan is installed in an opening in the top-floor ceiling, usually a central hallway, with either the fan mounted directly over the opening or, in newer ducted designs, the fan body sitting remotely in the attic and connected to a ceiling grille by insulated flexible duct. When it runs, it creates a slight negative pressure in the house, so outdoor air flows in through every open window. The air sweeps through the rooms, carrying heat from the occupants, furniture and walls, and is pushed into the attic. From there it leaves through the attic's soffit, gable and ridge vents.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because it replaces the indoor air with outdoor air, a whole house fan can never make the house cooler than it is outside. What it does is close the gap fast. After a hot day, walls, floors and furniture hold heat, and the house can stay several degrees warmer than the outside air well into the night. A whole house fan running at several thousand cubic feet per minute flushes that heat out, and by morning the structure itself has cooled, so the house starts the next day with a cooler thermal mass. Ceiling fans running at the same time add a wind-chill effect that makes the moving air feel cooler still.
          </p>

          <p>
            An attic fan is mounted on the roof deck, under a weatherproof hood, or behind a gable vent. It is normally switched by a thermostat set to start the fan when the attic passes a set temperature, commonly in the region of 100 degrees Fahrenheit or higher, and some models also have a humidistat. When it runs, it pulls air out of the attic and relies on intake vents, usually soffit vents, to supply replacement air. Solar-powered versions do the same job with a small photovoltaic panel and run only while the sun is up.
          </p>

          <p>
            The weakness of an attic fan is in the replacement air. If the attic's intake vents are small, blocked by insulation or simply not there, the fan still has to get its air from somewhere. It finds it through the paths of least resistance: the gaps around recessed lights, attic hatches, plumbing and wiring penetrations, top plates of interior walls and leaky ducts. Those paths lead straight down into your living space, which is the root of the attic fan debate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cfm-and-energy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. 3. CFM, Watts and Running Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whole house fans are large. Residential models run from roughly 1,500 CFM for small homes and single zones to well over 6,000 CFM for large houses, and several of the fans in our rankings sit in the 3,000 to 3,500 CFM range. California's energy code, which requires a whole house fan in new homes in some of its hotter climate zones, sets a minimum of 1.5 CFM per square foot of conditioned floor area. On a 2,000 square foot house that means at least 3,000 CFM, and many installers size larger for faster evening cooling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Attic fans are smaller because the volume they serve is smaller. Common roof-mount powered attic ventilators are rated at around 1,000 to 1,500 CFM, with Master Flow's ERV series, for example, rated at 1,000, 1,250 and 1,500 CFM for attics of up to about 1,600, 2,200 and 2,800 square feet respectively. Solar units tested by the Florida Solar Energy Center delivered about 600 to 800 CFM at peak sun. That is plenty to change attic air quickly, but it is a different job from replacing the air in a whole house.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Power Draw</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">250-300 W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Conventional powered attic fan, per FSEC</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">A few hundred W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Whole house fan, model and speed dependent</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Several kW</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Central air conditioner while running</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1.5 CFM/sq ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">California minimum whole house fan sizing</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            On energy, the whole house fan's case is about what it replaces. A whole house fan typically draws a few hundred watts, with efficient ducted models on low speed at the lower end, while a central air conditioner draws several thousand watts when its compressor runs. Every evening hour the fan cools the house instead of the air conditioner is a large saving, and flushing the heat out of the structure overnight can delay when the air conditioner needs to start the next afternoon. In a climate with cool evenings, that adds up across a season.
          </p>

          <p>
            The attic fan's case is about what it reduces, and the measured answer is: not much. The Florida Solar Energy Center found conventional powered attic ventilators often draw 250 to 300 watts, and in earlier testing it measured a fan drawing 284 watts while the cooling reduction it produced was estimated at about 170 watts, so the fan used more electricity than it saved. Its later test of a solar unit, which uses no grid power, found roughly a 6 percent cut in cooling energy at the test home, worth around 37 dollars a year, with a payback of more than twenty years at an installed cost of about 850 dollars.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason the attic fan saves so little is that in a well-insulated house, heat flowing down through the ceiling is a modest share of the total cooling load. Windows, walls, air leakage and the people and appliances inside matter more. Cooling the attic by a few degrees trims a small slice. The exception is a house with ducts or an air handler in the attic, where a hot attic heats the ducts directly, but sealing and insulating those ducts is the better fix and costs no electricity to run.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="attic-fan-debate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. 4. The Attic Fan Debate: Depressurisation and Conditioned Air
          </h2>

          <p>
            The case against powered attic ventilators is not that they fail to move air. It is that they move the wrong air. An attic fan lowers the air pressure in the attic relative to the house. If the ceiling between them were perfectly sealed, that would not matter. In real houses it is not. Recessed lights, hatches, pull-down stairs, bath fan housings, wiring holes, plumbing stacks, chimney chases and the tops of interior walls all connect the living space to the attic. A depressurised attic pulls air through every one of them.
          </p>

          <p>
            In an air-conditioned house, the air being pulled through those gaps is air you have just paid to cool and dehumidify. It goes into the attic and out of the roof, and the house replaces it by drawing in hot outdoor air through its own leaks. That is why several field studies, including the Florida Solar Energy Center's work, found powered attic ventilators saved little or nothing, and why building scientists commonly advise against them for conditioned homes. The fan can end up increasing the cooling load it was supposed to cut.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SAFETY ISSUE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A depressurised house can backdraft gas appliances.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If an attic fan pulls enough air out of the living space, it can reverse the draft of a gas water heater or furnace that vents by natural draft, drawing combustion gases into the house. The Florida Solar Energy Center flagged this as a potentially hazardous condition. If you have an atmospherically vented gas appliance, avoid powered attic fans or have the house tested.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The same depressurisation can pull other things in. In regions with radon, lowering the pressure in the house can increase soil gas entry. In a humid climate, pulling outdoor air in through wall cavities can carry moisture into places it condenses. None of this is guaranteed to happen in a given house, which is why you will find homeowners who are happy with their attic fans. But the risk is real, the measured savings are small, and the alternatives, which are sealing the ceiling, adding insulation and improving passive venting, carry none of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whole house fans create negative pressure too, which is why every manufacturer insists on open windows. The difference is that a whole house fan is designed to be run with a large, deliberate opening to the outdoors, so the house pressure stays close to neutral and the air comes in through the windows rather than up the flue. Run a whole house fan with the windows shut and it becomes a backdraft hazard of its own, at much higher airflow than an attic fan. Open windows first, every time, spread across the rooms you want cooled.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already have an attic fan, there are reasonable steps short of removing it. Make sure the intake venting is generous and unobstructed, with baffles keeping insulation clear of the soffits. Air-seal the attic floor, starting with the largest leaks around hatches, recessed lights and chases. Raise the thermostat setting so it runs only on the hottest afternoons, or disconnect it and let passive venting do the work. An attic fan near a ridge vent can also short-circuit, pulling air in through the nearby ridge vent instead of the soffits, so check how your vents are arranged.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="climate-fit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. 5. Climate Fit: Dry Evenings vs Muggy Nights
          </h2>

          <p>
            A whole house fan only cools when the outdoor air is cooler than the indoor air, so climate decides how many hours a year it is useful. It shines where days are hot and evenings cool quickly, which describes much of the western US: inland California, the intermountain West, the high desert and many mountain and northern regions. In those places outdoor temperatures commonly fall into the 60s or low 70s Fahrenheit by late evening, and a whole house fan can replace the air conditioner for much of the night.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is a weaker fit in hot, humid climates such as the Gulf Coast and much of the Southeast. Evenings there may be warm and close to saturated. Pulling that air through the house can lower the temperature a little while raising the indoor humidity, and the air conditioner then has to remove that moisture the next day, using some of the energy you thought you saved. Damp indoor air also feels warmer and can contribute to musty smells and mold. In those regions a whole house fan works in spring and autumn, but the summer case is limited.
          </p>

          <p>
            Air quality is the other climate question. Because a whole house fan brings large volumes of outdoor air inside, it also brings in pollen, dust and, in fire-prone regions, wildfire smoke. People with seasonal allergies may find a fan run on a high-pollen evening counterproductive, and anyone in a region with smoke seasons needs to treat it as a fan for clean-air evenings only. Window screens stop insects, not fine particles. The attic fan, by contrast, has no effect on indoor air quality unless it is depressurising the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Attic fans do not depend on climate in the same way, because they run during the day regardless of outdoor temperature. But their limited benefit is smallest in the climates where people most want them. In hot, humid regions, the houses most likely to have air conditioning running all day are also the ones most likely to lose conditioned air through a depressurised ceiling. In cold climates, an attic fan run with a humidistat to clear winter moisture can draw warm, moist house air into the attic, which is the opposite of what you want.
          </p>

          <p>
            A useful self-test for a whole house fan is an indoor-outdoor thermometer. Track temperatures for a couple of weeks in summer and see how many evenings outdoor air drops a few degrees below indoor air by 8 or 9 pm and stays there. If that happens most nights, a whole house fan will earn its keep. If outdoor air rarely drops below indoor before midnight, or the evenings are muggy, it will spend most of the season switched off. Pair either result with ceiling fans, which make any temperature feel cooler; our guide to <a href="/ceiling-fan-direction-summer-and-winter/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">ceiling fan direction for summer and winter</a> covers the setting that matters.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="attic-venting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. 6. Attic Venting: Net Free Area for Both Fans
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both fans depend on attic vents, but for opposite reasons. A whole house fan exhausts several thousand CFM into the attic, and that air has to leave through the exhaust and intake vents. The guidance used by the Department of Energy and by California's energy code is about one square foot of net free vent area for every 750 CFM of fan capacity. Net free area is the open area after louvres and screens are accounted for, often well under half the vent's physical size, so use the manufacturer's published figure rather than measuring the opening.
          </p>

          <p>
            That requirement is much larger than ordinary attic ventilation. Building codes typically require net free vent area of 1/150 of the attic floor area, or 1/300 when vents are balanced between high and low or a vapour retarder is installed in the coldest climate zones. A 1,500 square foot attic under the 1/300 rule needs 5 square feet. A 4,500 CFM whole house fan needs 6 square feet on its own, and the Department of Energy notes that a whole house fan may need two to four times the normal vent area. Under-vented, the fan pressurises the attic, delivers well below its rating and can push attic air back into the house.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fan</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Venting rule of thumb</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Example</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whole house fan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1 sq ft net free area per 750 CFM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4,500 CFM fan: about 6 sq ft of net free area</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Powered attic fan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Intake roughly 1 sq ft per 300 CFM (per manufacturer specs)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,200 CFM fan: about 4 sq ft of intake</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Passive attic, code minimum</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/150 of attic floor area, or 1/300 with balanced venting</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500 sq ft attic: 10 or 5 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A powered attic fan needs intake area rather than exhaust area, because the fan itself is the exhaust. Manufacturers specify it: Master Flow lists 480 square inches of intake net free area for its 1,000 CFM model and 720 square inches for its 1,500 CFM model, which works out to roughly one square foot of intake per 300 CFM. Without that intake, the fan pulls its replacement air from the house below, which is exactly the depressurisation problem described above. Many attics with older soffit vents choked by insulation fall well short.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical test for either fan is the same: go into the attic, list every vent, look up or estimate its net free area, and check that soffit vents are not buried under insulation. Fit baffles where they are. For a whole house fan, multiply your total net free area by 750, and that is the largest fan the attic can actually support. For an attic fan, if the intake falls short of the manufacturer's figure, add intake before anything else. If you need regular attic access for this kind of work, a proper <a href="/best-attic-ladder/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">attic ladder</a> makes it far less of a chore.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-noise-winter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. 7. Cost, Noise and Winter: The Practical Comparison
          </h2>

          <p>
            Attic fans are cheaper to buy and install. Cost guides commonly put an installed powered attic fan in the low hundreds to under a thousand dollars, with solar units at the upper end, while whole house fans commonly run from around 900 to 2,400 dollars installed, more for large or premium ducted systems or where extra attic venting has to be cut in. That gap narrows when you count what each one does. The whole house fan replaces hours of air conditioning; the attic fan, by the best field evidence, barely pays for itself, if at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Noise is where whole house fans earned their old reputation. Traditional direct-mount fans bolted over a ceiling opening put the motor and blades a few feet from the hallway, and the roar was the main reason people stopped using them. Ducted designs move the fan into the attic, several feet from the grille, with the duct absorbing much of the sound; on low speed the better ones are quiet enough to run overnight. Attic fans are mounted in the roof, so their noise is mostly outdoors and in the attic, although a cheap motor can still hum through a thin ceiling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Winter is the whole house fan's main weakness. The ceiling opening is a large hole between heated space and a cold attic. Traditional gravity louvres seal poorly and leak warm air all season, which in a cold climate can eat into the summer savings. Buy a fan with an insulated, sealing damper, or fit a rigid insulated cover each autumn. Attic fans add a roof penetration that needs good flashing, and their thermostat should be set so they never run in cold weather, when exhausting the attic can draw warm, moist house air upward.
          </p>

          <p>
            Putting it together, the decision for most homeowners is not whole house fan versus attic fan, but whole house fan versus nothing. If you have cool evenings, adequate attic venting or the willingness to add it, and a sensible spot in a central ceiling, a whole house fan is worth serious consideration, and our <a href="/best-whole-house-fan/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">whole house fan rankings</a> compare ducted and direct-mount models on airflow, noise and damper sealing. If your evenings are muggy, put the money into air sealing, insulation and efficient cooling instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever way you go, ceiling fans are the cheap partner that makes both cooling strategies feel better. A fan moving air across the room lets you raise the thermostat a few degrees without feeling warmer, and it helps a whole house fan's breeze reach the corners. If you are adding fans, our <a href="/what-size-ceiling-fan-do-i-need/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">ceiling fan sizing guide</a> covers blade span by room size, and our <a href="/dc-vs-ac-ceiling-fan/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">DC vs AC ceiling fan</a> comparison explains why a DC motor can be worth paying for in a fan you run every night.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an Attic Fan to Cool the House</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An attic fan only exhausts attic air. It never moves the air in your rooms, so it cannot cool them on a summer evening. If the goal is a cooler house without running air conditioning, you need a whole house fan, which draws outdoor air through open windows and the living space itself.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Whole House Fan With Windows Shut</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                With the house closed, the fan pulls its replacement air from wherever it can, including down a chimney or the flue of a gas water heater or furnace, which can draw combustion gases indoors. Open several windows wide before switching on, spread them across the rooms you want cooled, and never leave it running unattended after closing up.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Net Free Vent Area</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A whole house fan needs about one square foot of net free attic vent area per 750 CFM, often two to four times normal venting. Undersized vents pressurise the attic, choke the fan and can push attic air back indoors. Add up your vents' published net free area before choosing a fan size.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Installing an Attic Fan in a Leaky Ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A powered attic fan with too little intake pulls replacement air through recessed lights, hatches and wall top plates, drawing conditioned air out of the house. It can also backdraft natural-draft gas appliances. Seal the attic floor and confirm intake venting first, or skip the attic fan entirely.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Whole House Fan on Muggy Evenings</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Outdoor air that is cooler but very humid raises indoor humidity, which makes the house feel clammy and forces the air conditioner to remove that moisture later. Check outdoor dew point as well as temperature, and keep the whole house fan for evenings that are both cooler and reasonably dry.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a whole house fan and an attic fan?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A whole house fan is mounted in the ceiling and pulls outdoor air through open windows and the living space, exhausting it through the attic, so it cools the rooms. An attic fan is mounted in the roof or gable and exhausts only attic air. It lowers attic temperature somewhat but does not exchange any air in your living space.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a whole house fan better than an attic fan?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For cooling a house, yes, in a climate with cool, reasonably dry evenings. A whole house fan can replace hours of air conditioning, while field testing has found powered attic fans save little energy and can pull conditioned air out of the house. Attic fans are cheaper, but cheaper is not better if they barely help.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do attic fans actually save energy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually very little. The Florida Solar Energy Center measured a conventional attic fan drawing about 284 watts against an estimated cooling saving of about 170 watts, so it used more than it saved. A later solar unit test found roughly a 6 percent cooling reduction, with a payback of over twenty years at typical installed cost.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a whole house fan replace air conditioning?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In dry climates with cool evenings, it can replace the air conditioner for much of the night and delay when it needs to start the next day. It cannot cool the house below outdoor temperature, so on hot afternoons and humid nights you still need air conditioning. In muggy climates its useful season is mostly spring and autumn.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many CFM do I need for a whole house fan?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                California's energy code sets a minimum of 1.5 CFM per square foot of conditioned floor area, so a 2,000 square foot home needs at least 3,000 CFM, and many installers size larger. Then check attic venting: you need roughly one square foot of net free vent area for every 750 CFM of fan capacity.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need extra attic vents for a whole house fan?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes. A whole house fan needs about one square foot of net free vent area per 750 CFM, which can be two to four times the venting a typical attic has. Use the manufacturers' published net free area figures for your vents, check that soffits are not blocked by insulation, and add vent area before buying a larger fan.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I have both a whole house fan and an attic fan?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but there is little reason to. A whole house fan already flushes the attic each time it runs. A powered attic fan adds daytime electricity use with small measured savings, and if poorly coordinated it can compete for vent area. Most homes are better served by the whole house fan plus good passive attic venting.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

