        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A whole house fan and an attic fan solve different problems, and only one of them cools the rooms you live in. The whole house fan replaces warm indoor air with cool evening air, uses a fraction of the power of air conditioning, and flushes the attic as a bonus. The attic fan moves only attic air, saves little in measured tests, and can pull conditioned air and even combustion gases where they should not go.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If your evenings are cool and dry, size a whole house fan by floor area, confirm about one square foot of net free attic vent per 750 CFM, and choose a ducted model with a sealing damper. Our <a href="/best-whole-house-fan/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best whole house fans</a> are the place to start.
          </p>
